Project instructions for `{{PROJECT_NAME}}`.

**KISS. YAGNI.** Direct answers. Simplest thing that works. Don't build what isn't asked for.

## Hard Rules (always on)

1. **Two Strikes** — same op fails twice → STOP. Research, log the failure mode, report. No blind third retry. Trigger `five-whys` instead.
2. **Blocker Protocol** — hit a blocker, stop and report. Explain what was tried. Ask before working around.
3. **KISS / YAGNI** — Reuse before you write. Pick the simplest mechanism. Don't build for hypothetical futures.
4. **Read before writing** — understand existing context (file, schema, prior version) before producing output.
5. **No fabrication** — never invent quotes, citations, file paths, IDs, or APIs. If unsure, say so.
6. **Quality gate before irreversible decisions** — schema changes, MCP/agent architecture, public-facing copy, anything that compounds → run `quality-review`.
7. **Audit your writes** — after every write, restate what the tool returned (IDs, paths, params). No self-grading; cite the actual response.

## The Router

Every non-trivial request: **Classify → Load → Think → Pre-flight Checklist → Validate.** Full ritual: `.claude/rules/routing-protocol.md`.

| If request touches… | Load |
|---|---|
| A plan, design, PRD, architecture decision, or other proposal before commit | `quality-review` skill (parallel adversarial team) |
| Just KISS/YAGNI lens on existing code or design | `simplify` command |
| Just "challenge my reasoning" lens | `adversarial` command |
| Edge cases / failure modes / "what breaks this" | `chaos` command |
| Something failed unexpectedly, retry-blind temptation | `five-whys` skill |
| Client-facing prose, deliverables, emails ≥3 sentences | `writing-quality` skill |
| Validating a skill or agent against a target repo | `skill-validator` skill (ralph loop) |

Multi-domain → load multiple. Domain-specific governance (Notion, Gmail, Drive, Supabase, etc.) belongs in a project-specific rule file or Cowork plugin, NOT in this template.

## Agents

| Agent | Model | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `adversarial-reviewer` | Opus | Challenges claims and assumptions. Asks "is this the right thing?" |
| `simplifier` | Sonnet | Finds over-engineering. Asks "is this the simplest way?" |
| `chaos-engineer` | Sonnet | Stress-tests for edge cases and failure modes. Asks "what breaks this?" |

The three lenses are non-overlapping by design. `quality-review` runs all three in parallel and synthesizes the findings.

